<h2>Approach and Model Selection</h2>
<p><b>Approach.</b> Two parts for a {{ n('eda.k') }}-way, multilingual, open-set task. A closed-set classifier: multilingual-e5-base fine-tuned end to end (learning rate {{ n('a.lr') }}, batch {{ n('a.batch') }}, stopped at epoch {{ n('a.epoch') }}, seed {{ n('a.model_seed') }}) with temperature scaling; floors are B0 (character TF-IDF + LR) and B1 (frozen e5 + LR). And a post-hoc Mahalanobis open-set score on its penultimate features, thresholded on known-class validation rows only so that {{ n('srv.ret_target') }} of known rows are kept; messages below it go to an unknown path instead of a handler.</p>
<table class="bk">
<tr><th>Candidate (all <code>AutoModelForSequenceClassification</code>)</th><th class="num">CV macro-F1 (mean &plusmn; std)</th><th class="num">params (M)</th><th class="num">s / run</th></tr>
{% for r in bakeoff[2:] %}
<tr{% if r.epoch and loop.first %} class="ship"{% endif %}><td>{{ r.name }}</td><td class="num">{{ n(r.f1) }} &plusmn; {{ n(r.f1s) }}</td><td class="num">{{ c(r.params) }}</td><td class="num">{{ n(r.wall) }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}
</table>
<div class="cap">{{ M() }} {{ O() }} CV on train+val only, {{ n('bk.e5_lr3e-05.n') }} fold-runs for the best learning rate per model; test untouched. Full table: Appendix {{ sec.ablations }}.</div>
<p><b>Why e5-base.</b> The selection rules were fixed before the candidates were run: highest mean CV macro-F1, and a smaller or faster model within one std wins instead. e5-base has the highest CV macro-F1 ({{ n('bk.e5_lr3e-05.f1') }} &plusmn; {{ n('bk.e5_lr3e-05.f1s') }}); the others fall below its mean minus one std ({{ n('bk.win_threshold') }}) and no rival is smaller or faster, so the second rule does not trigger (best rival {{ n('bk.r2_gap') }} behind). Cost of the choice: the fp32 weights are {{ n('q.size.torch') }} MB{% if srv_colab %} and the shipped ONNX fp32 backend serves a batch-1 message on one CPU thread at p50 {{ n('srv.onnx_fp32.t1.p50') }} ms (measured on Colab){% else %}; CPU latency is measured in the Colab canonical run and filled at publish time{% endif %}.</p>
<p><b>Encoder versus LLM.</b> Local 8B instruction models ({{ llm_models }}, zero-shot and {{ n('llm.n_shots') }}-shot) on the same {{ n('a.n') }} test rows reach macro-F1 {{ n('llm.f1_min') }} to {{ n('llm.f1_max') }} against {{ n('a.f1') }} and almost never abstain (strict rejection at most {{ n('llm.rej_max') }} against {{ n('llm.ft_rej') }} at {{ n('llm.ft_ret') }} retention for the shipped scorer); {{ M() }} on the same local GPU they take p50 {{ n('llm.p50_min') }} to {{ n('llm.p50_max') }} ms per message against {{ n('ft.gpu1.p50_ms') }} ms for the encoder (at least {{ n('ratio.lat_gpu') }}&times;) and cost {{ n('llm.usd_min') }} to {{ n('llm.usd_max') }} per 1k messages against {{ n('ft.gpu1.usd') }} (at least {{ n('ratio.usd') }}&times;; {{ E() }} optimistic lower bound at {{ n('llm.gpu_hr') }} per hour). This GPU basis is the only like-for-like one: the encoder's CPU latency was measured on shared Colab vCPUs and no LLM was timed on CPU, so no CPU-versus-GPU ratio is formed (Appendix {{ sec.llm }}).</p>

<h2>Data Characteristics and Handling</h2>
<ul class="tight">
<li><b>Class imbalance.</b> Ratio {{ n('eda.imb') }} (largest class {{ n('eda.cc_max') }} rows, smallest {{ n('eda.cc_min') }}): macro-F1 and stratified, duplicate-grouped splits; class-weighted cross-entropy was ablated, not adopted (CV delta {{ n('abl.class_weight.delta') }}, p = {{ n('abl.class_weight.p') }}).</li>
<li><b>Multilingual.</b> Language ID ({{ M() }}) finds {{ n('eda.nonen') }} non-English by primary language, not the stated ~{{ n('eda.claim') }}; Spanish, French, German and Chinese slices are small ({{ n('eda.lang.es') }}, {{ n('eda.lang.fr') }}, {{ n('eda.lang.de') }}, {{ n('eda.lang.zh') }} rows), so each carries n and an interval.</li>
<li><b>Sibling classes.</b> The three <code>shipment_information.*</code> classes overlap lexically (nearest-neighbour accuracy {{ n('eda.sib_1nn') }}); a hierarchical auxiliary loss gave no gain over the CV noise band (delta {{ n('abl.hier_aux.delta') }}, p = {{ n('abl.hier_aux.p') }}); plain cross-entropy kept.</li>
<li><b>Noise.</b> {{ n('eda.short') }} rows are very short (emoji, one-liners) and kept; {{ n('eda.noisy') }} of rows are noisy ({{ n('eda.lower') }} all-lower-case, {{ n('eda.slang') }} slang); robustness is measured in Appendix {{ sec.robust }}.</li>
<li><b>Catch-all classes.</b> <code>chitchat</code> ({{ n('eda.cc.chitchat') }} rows) and <code>other</code> ({{ n('eda.cc.other') }} rows) are the smallest classes (test F1 {{ n('a.pc.chitchat.f1') }}, n = {{ n('a.pc.chitchat.support') }}; {{ n('a.pc.other.f1') }}, n = {{ n('a.pc.other.support') }}: small n, indicative).</li>
<li><b>Small data.</b> {{ n('eda.n') }} rows in total and a {{ n('a.n') }}-row test split, so one error moves a class F1 a lot; weight decay {{ n('a.wd') }} and the stopping epoch were chosen on CV, never on test, and headline numbers carry an interval.</li>
<li><b>ID-prefix shortcut.</b> PO-style IDs occur in {{ n('eda.sc.po.n') }} rows, {{ n('eda.sc.po.share') }} labelled <code>{{ t('eda.sc.po.top') }}</code>; LD-style IDs in {{ n('eda.sc.ld.n') }} rows, {{ n('eda.sc.ld.share') }} <code>{{ t('eda.sc.ld.top') }}</code>. Swapping the LD prefix for the neutral REF prefix flips {{ n('swap.LD->REF.rate') }} of LD rows (Appendix {{ sec.robust }}).</li>
</ul>

