<p class="small"><b>Candidate codes used in this appendix (each defined once).</b> A1 = random ID prefixes in training text; A2 = outlier exposure with generic synthetic out-of-scope text; A3 = machine-translated and noisy copies of training rows. I1 = alternative open-set scorers: i1a = relative Mahalanobis (class distance minus a class-agnostic background distance), i1b = L2-normalised Mahalanobis on the same weights. I4 = an ID-consistency loss (a symmetric KL between a row and its prefix-swapped twin), i4a and i4b being two loss weights. I6 = alternative thresholding: i6a = calibration rows grouped by predicted class, i6b = cross-fitted Gaussian. N1 = verified hard negatives as training signal; N2 = a fine-tuned e5-large. D1 = the oracle probe, D2 = ID-neutral Track B scoring, D3 = threshold stability, D4 = the data-scaling slope.</p>

<h2>Debrief Answers</h2>
<div class="box qa">
<p><b>Debrief answers.</b> One short paragraph for each question we expect in the walk-through.</p>
<p><b class="q">Why this metric, and what does accuracy hide?</b> Every intent routes to a different handler, so each of the {{ n('eda.k') }} classes must count equally: macro-F1 is the primary metric, accuracy the sanity number. On this mildly imbalanced data (largest class {{ n('eda.cc_max') }} rows, smallest {{ n('eda.cc_min') }}, ratio {{ n('eda.imb') }}) the two are close on test ({{ n('a.f1') }} vs {{ n('a.acc') }}), so accuracy hides little in aggregate but does hide per-class differences at {{ n('a.sup_min') }} to {{ n('a.sup_max') }} test rows per class. The lowest-scoring test class is <code>{{ t('a.worst.label') }}</code> (F1 {{ n('a.worst.f1') }}, support {{ n('a.worst.support') }}), noise-dominated at that n, hence the CV and CIs below.</p>
<p><b class="q">With {{ n('eda.n') }} rows, how do we trust the test number?</b> Not alone: its interval is wide ({{ n('a.f1') }}), so three estimates are reported with their roles. The single test split is the unbiased one; the {{ n('cv.n_runs') }}-run CV ({{ n('cv.f1_mean') }} &plusmn; {{ n('cv.f1_std') }}) and the out-of-fold score ({{ n('oof.f1') }}) are {{ O() }}, as the model and epoch were selected on them. Against baselines the fine-tune clearly beats B0 (paired macro-F1 difference {{ n('a.B0.delta') }}) but not provably B1 on test ({{ n('a.B1.delta') }}, the interval includes zero; exact McNemar p = {{ n('a.B1.mc_p') }}); in CV it beats B1 (Nadeau-Bengio p = {{ n('bk.nb_p_B1') }}).</p>
<p><b class="q">The model confuses realtime_query and disruptions: what now?</b> In the {{ n('err.oof45.n_runs') }} out-of-fold fold-runs these two siblings are confused {{ n('err.rtdi.rt_to_di') }} times (realtime as disruptions) and {{ n('err.rtdi.di_to_rt') }} times (the reverse), {{ n('err.rtdi.share') }} of all errors; on the test split {{ n('err.test_rtdi') }} of {{ n('a.n_errors') }} errors. The hypothesis that errors concentrate in the shipment family is rejected: the family is {{ n('err.oof45.fam_share_rows') }} of rows but {{ n('err.oof45.fam_share_err') }} of errors; real confusion sits in document_processing vs knowledge_base and customer_support (see section {{ sec.errors }}). Lexical overlap in the family is real (nearest-neighbour accuracy {{ n('eda.sib_1nn') }}), but a hierarchical auxiliary loss gave no gain over the CV noise band (delta {{ n('abl.hier_aux.delta') }}, p = {{ n('abl.hier_aux.p') }}); plain cross-entropy kept. Next: temporal-cue labelling rules and more disruption examples, not more modelling.</p>
<p><b class="q">A brand-new intent appears next quarter. What happens, and how do we add a 13th class?</b> The router abstains on {{ n('tb.headline.strict_recall_95') }} of unseen-intent messages at {{ n('tb.headline.retention_95') }} known retention, so about {{ n('tb.not_rej') }} of novel intents (headline holdout) are still routed to a known label (most often <code>{{ t('tb.land0.label') }}</code>). Per {{ n('biz.per') }} messages at {{ n(P5) }} novel-intent traffic and the {{ n(R95) }} operating point ({{ E() }} using the CONFIRM-mean strict rejection {{ n('biz.rej95') }}, not the headline rate above): {{ n('biz.r4.unknowns_caught_strict') }} caught, {{ n('biz.r4.unknowns_misrouted_strict') }} misrouted, {{ n('biz.r4.known_wrongly_abstained') }} known messages wrongly abstained. The extension path (cluster abstained traffic, label, kNN bank, retrain, shadow, promote) is in section {{ sec.production }}.</p>
<p><b class="q">Why is softmax accuracy zero on a class it never saw, and how did we measure open-set behaviour?</b> It can only emit one of its trained labels, so accuracy on a held-out class is zero by construction. We therefore hold out whole classes (headline: {{ t('tb.holdout') }}; plus {{ n('tb.loco_n') }} leave-one-class-out runs), set the threshold on known-only validation data at fixed retention, and report rejection recall, retention and AUROC. MSP reaches AUROC {{ n('tb.m.msp.auroc') }} on the headline holdout; the shipped Mahalanobis scorer {{ n('tb.m.maha_ft.auroc') }}.</p>
<p><b class="q">Does the metric hold on the non-English slice?</b> The stated share of about {{ n('eda.claim') }} non-English is not what the data show: language ID finds {{ n('eda.nonen') }}. On the {{ n('sl.oof.non_english_defA.n') }} non-English out-of-fold rows macro-F1 is {{ n('sl.oof.non_english_defA.f1') }} (wide interval); the test split has {{ n('sl.test.non_english_defA.n') }} rows, indicative. Under machine translation of English rows accuracy drops from {{ n('tr.oof.es.english_accuracy_same_rows') }} to {{ n('tr.oof.zh.accuracy') }} for Chinese, the worst language ({{ M() }}, MT evaluation data, see Appendix {{ sec.robust }}).</p>
<p><b class="q">How do we monitor and catch data and label drift?</b> Abstention rate (test reference: {{ n('sel.n_accepted') }} of {{ n('a.n') }} rows accepted), confidence and energy distributions, language mix, kNN distance to train; label audits by multi-model consensus did not meet our own calibration gate (judge accuracy {{ n('aud2.llama3') }} to {{ n('aud2.gemma3') }} against {{ n('aud2.gate') }}), so humans label the sample. Detail in section {{ sec.production }} and Appendix {{ sec.more }}.</p>
</div>

