<h2>Diagnostics (Oracle Probe, ID-Neutral Scoring, Threshold Stability, Learning Curve)</h2>
<p><b>Measured gap.</b> {{ M() }} On the headline holdout ({{ t('tb.holdout') }}) the shipped scorer has AUROC {{ n('tb.headline.auroc') }} but rejects {{ n('tb.headline.strict_recall_95') }} of unknown-intent rows at {{ n('tb.headline.retention_95') }} known retention (calibration target {{ n(R95) }}); on the {{ n('tb.confirm_n') }} CONFIRM classes rejection is {{ n('tb.confirm.strict_recall_95') }}. {{ n('tb.not_rej') }} of unknowns are therefore routed to a known label, and {{ n('tb.nr_safe') }} of the {{ n('tb.nr_total') }} un-rejected unknown rows land in a catch-all class. At the {{ n(R95) }} operating point and {{ n(P5) }} novel-intent traffic per {{ n('biz.per') }} messages that is {{ n('biz.r4.unknowns_misrouted_strict') }} misrouted, computed with the CONFIRM-mean strict rejection {{ n('biz.rej95') }} rather than the headline-holdout rate quoted above ({{ E() }}).</p>
<p><b>Root cause: facts from the Open-set improvement round diagnostics</b> (report-only, never selection inputs; tables below).</p>
<ul>
<li><b>D1, ORACLE separability.</b> Held-out intents are linearly separable in the fine-tuned feature space (oracle AUROC {{ n(p6a.d1.rows[0].unseen) }} on the unseen-known pool), yet unsupervised scoring reaches {{ n(p6a.d1.maha) }}; the gap, {{ n(p6a.d1.headroom) }}, is a missing negative training signal, which verified hard-negative OOS data (next step) supplies. The probe is trained on the held-out-class labels (headline holdout; pool = {{ n(p6a.d1.n_pos) }} unknown and {{ n(p6a.d1.n_neg) }} known rows the encoder never trained on), so the oracle AUROC is an upper bound on separability, not achievable rejection. Pools: the oracle figure drops the known training rows (the v1 encoder trained on them, which inflates the all-rows figure; that figure is in the D1 table below, marked as inflated); the unsupervised figure is v1's seed-mean Mahalanobis AUROC on the Track B evaluation rows, which the encoder also never trained on.</li>
<li><b>D2, ID-neutral Track B</b> (IDs rewritten at scoring time; headline). v1 strict rejection {{ n(p6a.d2.rows[0].raw_rej) }} raw, {{ n(p6a.d2.rows[0].neu_rej) }} neutral. v3 minus v1 in rejection: {{ n(p6a.d2.raw_rej_delta) }} raw, {{ n(p6a.d2.neu_rej_delta) }} neutral; ranking reversal: {{ t(p6a.d2.reversal) }}. v1's Track B lead over v3 therefore holds in direction under ID-neutral scoring, so the ranking is not an ID-shortcut artifact; the neutral lead is smaller and its interval reaches zero, so part of the raw gap is ID text and the neutral lead is a direction, not a significant gap. The v1 decision stands.</li>
<li><b>D3, threshold stability</b> ({{ n(p6a.d3.n_boot) }} resamples of the known calibration rows, {{ n(p6a.d3.n_seeds) }} seeds pooled). Retention sd {{ n(p6a.d3.ret_sd) }} ({{ n(p6a.d3.level) }} range {{ n(p6a.d3.ret_lo) }} to {{ n(p6a.d3.ret_hi) }}); unknown rejection sd {{ n(p6a.d3.rej_sd) }} ({{ n(p6a.d3.level) }} range {{ n(p6a.d3.rej_lo) }} to {{ n(p6a.d3.rej_hi) }}). On the evaluation rows the cross-fitted threshold gives retention {{ n(p6a.d3.cf_ret) }} and rejection {{ n(p6a.d3.cf_rej) }} (validation threshold: table below): it collapses known retention, so thresholds must be calibrated on the shipped model's own held-out data.</li>
<li><b>D4, learning curve</b> ({{ n(p6a.d4.rows[0].n) }} to {{ n(p6a.d4.rows[-1].n) }} training rows, {{ n(p6a.d4.f_lo) }} to {{ n(p6a.d4.f_hi) }} of the data; rows below). {{ M() }} Slope per doubling of the training data (CI over {{ n(p6a.d3.n_seeds) }} seeds, coarse): AUROC {{ n(p6a.d4.auroc_slope) }}, strict rejection {{ n(p6a.d4.rej_slope) }}. <b>ESTIMATE beyond {{ n(p6a.d4.rows[-1].n) }} rows</b>, assuming AUROC stays log-linear in rows (fit {{ n(p6a.d4.intercept) }} at {{ n(p6a.d4.rows[-1].n) }} rows plus the slope per doubling; interval from the slope CI only): {% for e in p6a.d4.est %}{{ n(e.n) }} rows ({{ n(e.mult) }}&times;) {{ n(e.auroc) }}{% if not loop.last %}; {% endif %}{% endfor %}. The line would hit the AUROC ceiling after {{ n(p6a.d4.sat) }} doublings, so the real curve must bend before then; the seed CI is coarse and no data beyond {{ n(p6a.d4.rows[-1].n) }} rows exists.</li>
</ul>
