<h2>LLM Baseline</h2>
<p>{{ M() }} Four local 8B configurations ({{ llm_models }}; zero-shot and {{ n('llm.n_shots') }}-shot) on the {{ n('a.n') }} test rows reach macro-F1 {{ n('llm.f1_min') }} to {{ n('llm.f1_max') }} against {{ n('llm.ft_f1') }} for the fine-tuned v1; on the headline holdout their strict rejection is at most {{ n('llm.rej_max') }} against {{ n('llm.ft_rej') }} for the shipped scorer. {{ E() }} At {{ n('llm.usd_min') }} to {{ n('llm.usd_max') }} per 1k messages (optimistic lower bound) they cost at least {{ n('ratio.usd') }}&times; the encoder's {{ n('ft.gpu1.usd') }} (same GPU basis); the full table is below.</p>
<p><b>Why an encoder fine-tune and not an LLM.</b> We measured the alternative: local 8B instruction models ({{ llm_models }}, zero-shot and with {{ n('llm.n_shots') }} few-shot examples) on the same {{ n('a.n') }} test rows (all numbers {{ M() }}; costs {{ E() }}). <i>Quality:</i> LLM macro-F1 is {{ n('llm.f1_min') }} to {{ n('llm.f1_max') }} against {{ n('a.f1') }}; the best LLM configuration ({{ llm_best.name }}) minus v1 is {{ n(llm_best.delta) }} (paired bootstrap). <i>Abstention:</i> the LLMs almost never answer unknown (at most {{ n('llm.rej_max') }} strict rejection on the headline holdout, {{ n(llm_best.ret) }} retention for the best) against {{ n('llm.ft_rej') }} at {{ n('llm.ft_ret') }} retention for the shipped scorer. <i>Latency:</i> p50 {{ n('llm.p50_min') }} to {{ n('llm.p50_max') }} ms per message on the local GPU against {{ n('ft.gpu1.p50_ms') }} ms for the encoder on the same GPU (at least {{ n('ratio.lat_gpu') }}&times;). <i>Cost:</i> {{ n('llm.usd_min') }} to {{ n('llm.usd_max') }} per 1k messages on a T4-class stand-in at {{ n('llm.gpu_hr') }} per hour (optimistic, a lower bound) against {{ n('ft.gpu1.usd') }} for the encoder on the same basis (at least {{ n('ratio.usd') }}&times;, the GPU latency ratio, as the hourly price is shared). {% if srv_colab %}No CPU-versus-GPU ratio is formed: the encoder's CPU latency (ONNX fp32, one thread, p50 {{ n('srv.onnx_fp32.t1.p50') }} ms) was measured on shared Colab vCPUs, whereas the LLM latencies above were measured on the local {{ t('llm.gpu_name') }}, and no LLM was timed on CPU, so the GPU comparison above is the only like-for-like one.{% else %}CPU latency of the encoder is not compared here: it is measured in the Colab canonical run and filled at publish time.{% endif %} <i>Where the LLM has the advantage (outside these measurements):</i> it needs no labelled training data and takes a new intent through the prompt, while the encoder needs labels and a retrain (or the kNN-bank shortcut in section {{ sec.production }}). Scope: local open-weight models only, no frontier API.</p>
