<h3>Data quirks, full list</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Fewer non-English rows than stated.</b> Language ID ({{ M() }} lingua, offline) finds {{ n('eda.nonen') }} non-English by primary language (code-mixed {{ n('eda.mixed') }}), not the stated ~{{ n('eda.claim') }}; {{ n('eda.undet') }} rows are undetermined and never counted. Spanish {{ n('eda.lang.es') }}, French {{ n('eda.lang.fr') }}, German {{ n('eda.lang.de') }}, Chinese {{ n('eda.lang.zh') }} rows: small slices, so each carries n and an interval.</li>
<li><b>Short and noisy text.</b> {{ n('eda.short') }} rows are very short (emoji, one-liners): kept. {{ n('eda.noisy') }} of rows are noisy ({{ n('eda.lower') }} all-lower-case, {{ n('eda.slang') }} slang). Token p99 is {{ n('eda.p99_tok') }}; max length {{ n('eda.maxlen') }}.</li>
<li><b>Near-duplicates.</b> {{ n('eda.dup_pairs') }} pairs above character-TF-IDF cosine {{ n('eda.dup_thr') }}, same label, are grouped so they never straddle splits or folds; cross-label pairs above {{ n('eda.dup_cross_thr') }}: {{ n('eda.dup_cross') }}.</li>
<li><b>Class imbalance.</b> Ratio {{ n('eda.imb') }}: macro-F1 and stratified, duplicate-grouped splits; class-weighted cross-entropy was ablated, not adopted (CV delta {{ n('abl.class_weight.delta') }}, p = {{ n('abl.class_weight.p') }}).</li>
<li><b>Catch-all classes.</b> <code>chitchat</code> ({{ n('eda.cc.chitchat') }} rows) and <code>other</code> ({{ n('eda.cc.other') }} rows) are the smallest classes (test F1 {{ n('a.pc.chitchat.f1') }}, n = {{ n('a.pc.chitchat.support') }}; {{ n('a.pc.other.f1') }}, n = {{ n('a.pc.other.support') }}: small n, indicative). In Track B lenient mode they count as safe landings, but {{ n('tb.nr_safe') }} of {{ n('tb.nr_total') }} un-rejected unknowns land there, so lenient equals strict.</li>
<li><b>Small data.</b> Weight decay {{ n('a.wd') }} and the stopping epoch ({{ n('a.epoch') }}) were chosen on CV, never on test; headline numbers carry a CV or bootstrap interval.</li>
<li><b>ID-prefix shortcut.</b> PO-style IDs occur in {{ n('eda.sc.po.n') }} rows, {{ n('eda.sc.po.share') }} labelled <code>{{ t('eda.sc.po.top') }}</code> (flagged: {{ t('eda.sc.po.risk') }}); LD-style IDs in {{ n('eda.sc.ld.n') }} rows, {{ n('eda.sc.ld.share') }} <code>{{ t('eda.sc.ld.top') }}</code>. A masked-entity ablation reduced CV F1 ({{ n('abl.entity_mask.delta') }}); the counterfactual damage is in Appendix {{ sec.robust }}.</li>
<li><b>Sibling overlap and label ambiguity.</b> The three shipment classes overlap lexically (nearest-neighbour accuracy {{ n('eda.sib_1nn') }}). An LLM label-ambiguity audit ({{ M() }}, {{ n('aud2.n') }} items, Fleiss kappa {{ n('aud2.kappa') }}) did not meet its pre-registered calibration gate (judge accuracy {{ n('aud2.qwen3') }} / {{ n('aud2.llama3') }} / {{ n('aud2.gemma3') }} vs {{ n('aud2.gate') }}), so its labels are not used and nothing is relabelled. Of {{ n('aud2.err_n') }} model errors the judges' consensus sided with gold in {{ n('aud2.err_gold') }}, the model in {{ n('aud2.err_model') }}, a third label in {{ n('aud2.err_other') }}, none in {{ n('aud2.err_none') }}.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Evaluation protocol</h3>
<p><b>Why macro-F1, CV and CIs.</b> Classes are equal-weight for routing, and the test split is {{ n('a.n') }} rows (about {{ n('a.sup_min') }} to {{ n('a.sup_max') }} per class), so one error moves a class F1 a lot; we pair the split with CV and intervals. <b>Splits.</b> Stratified and grouped by near-duplicate group: train {{ n('a.n_train') }}, validation {{ n('a.n') }}, test {{ n('a.n') }}; CV runs on the remaining {{ n('oof.n') }} rows. <b>Leakage controls.</b> Temperature and the abstention threshold are fitted on validation only; Track B thresholds use known-class calibration rows only; the audit of the {{ n('aud.n_runs') }} Track B runs found all assertions passing: {{ t('aud.ok') }} (zero held-out-class rows in any training set).</p>
<h3>What to monitor and how drift is caught</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Input and output drift:</b> abstention rate (test reference: {{ n('srv.cov') }} coverage), confidence and negative-energy distributions, language mix against the measured {{ n('eda.nonen') }} non-English, kNN distance to train compared with a reference window (PSI or MMD), per-intent volume, downstream handler failure rate.</li>
<li><b>Label drift:</b> weekly sampled audits labelled by consensus of different model families, blind, with agreement measured and the result labelled consensus, not ground truth. Our pilot ({{ n('aud2.n') }} items) had Fleiss kappa {{ n('aud2.kappa') }} but did not meet calibration (reliable: {{ t('aud2.reliable') }}), so humans label any sample that triggers a retrain.</li>
<li><b>13th-class playbook:</b> cluster the abstained traffic, name and label the cluster, add the class immediately through the kNN bank (no retraining), then retrain fully, shadow-evaluate against v1, and promote. Retraining triggers: sustained rise in abstention rate, kNN-distance PSI above its alert level, a handler failure spike, or a new labelled cluster of meaningful size.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Other limitations</h3>
<p>v2 and v3 lowered strict rejection on the scored Track B holdout and are not shipped; the LLM label audit did not meet its calibration gate and is not used. Small data: a {{ n('a.n') }}-row test split, language slices as small as {{ n('eda.lang.zh') }} rows (Chinese), MT-evaluated translation rows. <b>ID-prefix shortcut:</b> the neutral prefix <code>REF</code> still flips {{ n('swap.LD->REF.rate') }} of the {{ n('swap.LD->REF.n') }} LD-prefixed OOF rows ({{ n('swap.LD->REF.flips') }} flips; {{ n('ax.v1.flip') }} pooled over the neutral swaps in the multi-axis selection axes), so part of the closed-set accuracy rides on ID text. {% if srv_colab %}CPU latency was measured on Colab shared vCPUs (environment stated), not on an idle machine of our own.{% else %}CPU latency is measured in the Colab canonical run and filled at publish time.{% endif %}</p>
<div class="box gap3">
<p><b>{{ gap.box_title }}.</b> The core pipeline (EDA, a stratified split, an e5 fine-tune, macro-F1, an MSP abstention threshold set on held-out data, W&amp;B tracking, the Hub push) produces the Track A result: test macro-F1 {{ n('a.f1') }}.</p>
<p>The additional effort bought evidence rather than score: confidence intervals, pre-registered selection rules, and the Track B diagnosis (D1 to D4) with its fix plan.</p>
<p>That evidence changes what can be claimed: the gap to B1 ({{ n('a.B1.delta') }}) sits inside its interval at n = {{ n('a.n') }} rows and {{ n('tb.not_rej') }} of unseen intents are still routed to a known label, neither of which a point estimate alone shows.</p>
</div>
<h3>Next steps, detail</h3>
<p>{% if not srv_colab %}{{ L() }} Run the Colab latency cell (notebook section 8b) and add its JSON to the results. {% endif %}{{ L() }} <b>N1, verified hard-negative out-of-scope data (the first step).</b> D1 is the evidence: held-out intents are separable (oracle AUROC {{ n(p6a.d1.rows[0].unseen) }}) but the unsupervised score reaches {{ n(p6a.d1.maha) }}, a missing negative training signal. Generate out-of-scope messages that reuse keywords of the {{ n('eda.k') }} intents, keep those all three judges unanimously label as fitting none, then compare a K-plus-one class head against outlier exposure with a uniform target on the same Track B holdouts. Generic synthetic outlier exposure (A2) did not meet the Track A guard ({{ n('hist.guard.a2') }} vs {{ n('hist.guard.thr') }}) and lowered DEV AUROC ({{ n('hist.a2.auroc') }}); the judges did not meet calibration (reliable: {{ t('aud2.reliable') }}) and need a gate first. Published outlier-exposure results are outside these measurements. {{ L() }} <b>More labelled data</b> (D4): the measured AUROC slope is {{ n(p6a.d4.auroc_slope) }} per doubling, but the rejection slope ({{ n(p6a.d4.rej_slope) }}) has an interval reaching zero. {{ L() }} <b>I4, ID-consistency loss, with its measured trade-off:</b> neutral-swap flip rate {{ n('p6a.ax.i4a.c') }} and {{ n('p6a.ax.i4b.c') }} (two loss weights; negative is fewer flips) against CV macro-F1 {{ n('p6a.ax.i4a.a_ci') }} and {{ n('p6a.ax.i4b.a_ci') }}, ineligible on the F1 axis; it needs a variant that recovers that loss. v3 cut neutral-swap flips by {{ n('ax.a1a3.c') }} but moved headline rejection by {{ n('trd.head.rej95') }}, so any such attempt must be guarded on Track B too. {{ L() }} <b>N2, e5-large fine-tune: not worth it under D1 (cut).</b> On the all-rows pool, the pre-registered rule's metric (frozen encoders trained on none of these rows), frozen e5-large oracle AUROC is {{ n(p6a.n2.large) }} against {{ n(p6a.n2.base) }} for e5-base, a gap of {{ n(p6a.n2.diff) }}, short of the {{ n(p6a.n2.margin) }} the rule needs (run N2: {{ t(p6a.n2.run) }}). {{ L() }} <b>Closed in the open-set improvement round.</b> I1: i1a changed raw DEV rejection by {{ n('p6a.ax.i1a.b_raw_rej') }} (ineligible); i1b was selected, then held back by the pre-ship check (headline strict rejection {{ n('p6a.ship.raw.headline.rej') }}). I6: i6a retains {{ n('p6a.ax.i6a.ret_raw') }} and i6b {{ n('p6a.ax.i6b.ret_raw') }} of known DEV rows (collapsed).</p>

